1
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,000
The damndest thing you ever saw.

2
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Nashville is a drama, comedy, music film directed by Robert Altman.

3
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,000
The intersecting stories of twenty-four characters—from country star to wannabe to
reporter to waitress—connect to the music business in Nashville, Tennessee.

4
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:42,000
Norman, played by David Arkin.

5
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Lady Pearl, played by Barbara Baxley.

6
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:30,000
Delbert Reese, played by Ned Beatty.

7
00:44:48,000 --> 00:44:54,000
Connie White, played by Karen Black.

8
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:18,000
Barbara Jean, played by Ronee Blakley.

9
00:57:36,000 --> 00:57:42,000
Tommy Brown, played by Timothy Brown.

10
01:04:00,000 --> 01:04:06,000
Nashville is a 1975 American satirical musical comedy drama
film directed and produced by Robert Altman.

11
01:10:24,000 --> 01:10:30,000
The film follows various people involved in the country and gospel music industry in Nashville, Tennessee, over the five-day
period leading up to a gala concert for a populist outsider running for president on the Replacement Party ticket.

12
01:16:48,000 --> 01:16:54,000
Nashville is often noted for its scope; the film contains 24 main characters,
an hour's worth of musical numbers, and multiple storylines.

13
01:23:12,000 --> 01:23:18,000
Its large ensemble cast includes David Arkin, Barbara Baxley, Ned Beatty, Karen Black, Ronee Blakley, Timothy Brown, Keith Carradine, Geraldine Chaplin,
Robert DoQui, Shelley Duvall, Allen Garfield, Henry Gibson, Scott Glenn, Jeff Goldblum, Barbara Harris, David Hayward, Michael Murphy, Allan F.

14
01:29:36,000 --> 01:29:42,000
Nicholls, Dave Peel, Cristina Raines, Bert Remsen, Lily
Tomlin, Gwen Welles, and Keenan Wynn.

15
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:06,000
The screenplay for Nashville was written by Altman's frequent collaborator Joan Tewkesbury, based partly on
her experiences as an outsider visiting the city and observing its local music industry.

16
01:42:24,000 --> 01:42:30,000
Several incidents she experienced appear in the finished film, though Altman
improvised numerous additional scenes and plot strands during filming.

17
01:48:48,000 --> 01:48:54,000
The film was shot on location in Nashville in 1974.

18
01:55:12,000 --> 01:55:18,000
Nashville was released by Paramount Pictures in the summer of 1975, and opened with widespread critical
acclaim, praising the ensemble cast (mainly Blakley and Tomlin), mise-en-scène and soundtrack.

19
02:01:36,000 --> 02:01:42,000
It garnered numerous accolades, including five Academy Award nominations, including Best Picture, Best Director, Best Supporting
Actress (for both Ronee Blakley and Lily Tomlin), and winning for Best Original Song for Carradine's track "I'm Easy".

20
02:08:00,000 --> 02:08:06,000
The film was nominated for a total of 11 Golden Globe Awards, to date
the highest number of nominations received by one film.

21
02:14:24,000 --> 02:14:30,000
Since then, it has been considered Altman's magnum opus,
and one of the greatest films of all time.

22
02:20:48,000 --> 02:20:54,000
In 1992, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

23
02:27:12,000 --> 02:27:18,000
The film explores themes of country music, satire, music
festival, candidate, nashville, tennessee.

24
02:33:36,000 --> 02:33:42,000
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
